course: dictionary
lesson: 3122
title: Electricity, a word with many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Electricity is a noun, and it is uncountable, so we say «some electricity», not «an electricity». It names a kind of energy that flows through wires and powers lights, machines and devices. You cannot see it, so the words around it give you clues about what it is doing.
explain: Read «The electricity went off.» Here electricity means the supply of power to a home, so the lights and the fridge stopped. Read the words around the word, find the meaning that fits, then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Electricity also happens in nature. Lightning is electricity, and so is static electricity, like the crackle you hear when you pull off a jumper. The context tells you which meaning is meant: a power cut is about the supply, while a flash in the sky is about nature.
explain: The sentences about electricity often warn you about danger. «Water and electricity are dangerous together» is a safety message, not just a fact. Keep water away from electrical things, never poke anything into a socket, and stay away from fallen wires.
fact: Electricity is a flow of tiny charged particles through a material.
fact: Electricity only flows when it has a complete loop to go round, from the battery, through the bulb and back again. That loop is called a circuit.
fact: Metals conduct electricity, while plastic and rubber do not. Materials that stop electricity flowing are called insulators.
fact: Water with tiny bits of other things mixed in it, like tap water, can conduct electricity, so keep water away from electrical things.
fact: A battery pushes electricity round a circuit until its chemicals are used up. A charger can push the chemicals of a rechargeable battery back.
exercise: «The electricity went off.» What does electricity mean here? | A bright spark of lightning in the sky | *The supply of power to the home, so the lights stopped | The lights stopped because the power supply to the home was cut | A shiny metal wire on a table
exercise: What is a circuit? | *A complete loop that electricity flows round, from the battery, through the bulb and back again | A sudden crackle when you pull off a jumper | Something you can see on a screen | A kind of plastic that stops electricity
exercise: Which material is an insulator, one that does not let electricity flow? | Tap water | Copper wire | *Rubber
test: Which safety rule is right? | Put water in the socket to cool it | *Keep water away from electrical things | Fly a kite near power lines | Touch a fallen wire to see if it still works
test: In «static electricity», what does electricity mean? | A battery | The supply of power to homes | *Energy in nature, like the crackle when you pull off a jumper | The bulb in a lamp
exam: «There was a power cut in the street.» What does power cut mean? | Lightning strikes a tall tree | A battery runs out of chemicals | *The supply of electricity to the homes stops | Metal wires start to conduct electricity well
